Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>6  Finding and sorting</H1>
<HR>
An address book is only useful if you can find people in it. In this chapter the Find field learns to look at
every detail of a contact, not just the name, and the list learns to sort itself three ways - by surname,
first name or company - chosen from the menu or with F6, as the Style Guide suggests. On the way the searching
moves out of the window and into the Book, which is where it should have been all along. The result is
<CODE>!Contacts6</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Rules belong in the model</H2>
Chapter 5's Find field did its searching in MainWindow's <CODE>refresh</CODE>. That was quick to write, but
think about what else will soon want to search or sort the contacts: exporting them (chapter 8), the
organiser's Address section (chapter 10), and the organiser's Find, which searches every section at once
(chapter 16). Each would need its own copy of
the rules, and the copies would drift apart. And the rules are hard to test while they're inside a window:
Check can't open windows.<P>
So the rules for which contacts match and what order they go in move into the Book. The Book's
<CODE>list</CODE> method, which used to return every contact in surname order, now takes the search text and
the sort order and returns just the contacts to show, in order:<P>
<PRE>
  /** The contacts to show: those matching search, in order. */
  list({ search = '', sort = 'last' } = {}) {
    const s = search.toLowerCase();
    const found = this.all().filter((c) =&gt; !s || matches(c, s));
    return found.sort((a, b) =&gt; compare(a, b, sort));
  }
</PRE>
Anything that wants a list of contacts asks the Book for one, and gets the same answer. The window just shows
it:<P>
<PRE>
  refresh() {
    const sort = this.app.prefs.sort;
    const search = this.search;
    this.list.setItems(this.book.list({ search, sort }));
    this.shade();
  }
</PRE>
This is the same idea as chapter 4's, from the other side. There, the model told the views when it changed;
here, the views ask the model what to show, rather than working it out themselves. Windows show things and
pass on what the user does; the model knows the rules.<P>

<H3>Named arguments with defaults</H3>
<CODE>list</CODE> takes an object - <CODE>book.list({ search: 'smi', sort: 'first' })</CODE> - rather than
two arguments in a fixed order. Its parameter list takes the object apart:<P>
<PRE>
list({ search = '', sort = 'last' } = {}) {
</PRE>
<CODE>{ search, sort }</CODE> in a parameter list is <I>destructuring</I>: it makes two variables,
<CODE>search</CODE> and <CODE>sort</CODE>, from the properties of the object passed in. <CODE>= ''</CODE> and
<CODE>= 'last'</CODE> are defaults, used for a property that isn't given, and the <CODE>= {}</CODE> at the
end is the default for the whole object, so <CODE>book.list()</CODE> still works and means "everyone, by
surname". Named arguments like these read well at the call, and adding a new one later (a
<CODE>category</CODE>, say) doesn't disturb the old calls.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Searching every field</H2>
A contact matches if <I>any</I> of its details contains the search text:<P>
<PRE>
// a contact matches if any of its fields contains the search text
const matches = (c, s) =&gt; Object.entries(c).some(([k, v]) =&gt;
  k !== 'id' &amp;&amp; String(v).toLowerCase().includes(s));
</PRE>
<CODE>Object.entries(c)</CODE> turns a contact into an array of <CODE>[name, value]</CODE> pairs:
<CODE>[['first', 'Ann'], ['last', 'Smith'], ..., ['id', 1]]</CODE> (in the order the properties were made, so
the id, added last by the Book, comes last). <CODE>some</CODE> is an array method
that asks a question of each item in turn and is true as soon as one says yes (the opposite way round from
the first book's <CODE>filter</CODE>, which keeps every item that says yes). The question is written as an arrow
function whose parameter is itself taken apart: <CODE>([k, v]) =&gt;</CODE> destructures each pair into
<CODE>k</CODE>, the name, and <CODE>v</CODE>, the value. The id is left out (searching for <I>3</I> shouldn't
find contact number 3), and <CODE>String(v)</CODE> makes sure the value is text before it's searched.<P>
Now typing <CODE>friends</CODE> finds everyone in the Friends category, and a postcode or part of a phone
number finds its contact too:<P>
