<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Sorting</H2>
The first book sorted arrays with a <I>compare function</I>: <CODE>sort((a, b) =&gt; ...)</CODE> calls it with two
items, and it returns a negative number if <CODE>a</CODE> should come first, a positive one if
<CODE>b</CODE> should, and 0 if it doesn't matter. The Book's comparison has more to do: it must follow the
chosen order, and when two contacts tie (two Smiths), fall back on something else:<P>
<PRE>
// sort by the chosen field, then by name
function compare(a, b, sort) {
  const key = (c) =&gt; (sort === 'first' ? [c.first, c.last]
    : sort === 'company' ? [c.company, c.last, c.first]
    : [c.last || c.company, c.first]);
  const ka = key(a), kb = key(b);
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; ka.length; i++) {
    const d = (ka[i] ?? '').localeCompare(kb[i] ?? '', 'en',
      { sensitivity: 'base' });
    if (d) return d;
  }
  return a.id - b.id;
}
</PRE>
<CODE>key</CODE> gives, for a contact, the list of things to compare, most important first: for the surname
order, the surname (or the company, for a firm with no surname) and then the first name. The chain of
<CODE>? :</CODE> picks the list for the chosen order. The loop compares the two contacts' lists one item at a
time, and returns at the first difference; only if everything is the same does it fall back on the ids, so
that the order is always the same from one refresh to the next and the list never jumps about.<P>
Each pair of texts is compared with <CODE>localeCompare</CODE>, as in chapter 1, dictionary fashion and
taking no notice of capitals or accents. This time it's also given <CODE>'en'</CODE>, the language whose
dictionary order to use (English), so the order is the same whatever language the computer is set to.
<CODE>?? ''</CODE> turns a missing detail into empty text, which comes first: that's why, in first-name
order, Jam Tomorrow Cafe (which has no first name) is at the top.<P>
The orders the Book knows are kept in one place, with their names for the menu:<P>
<PRE>
export const SORTS = {
  last: 'Surname', first: 'First name', company: 'Company',
};
</PRE>

<H3>Testing it</H3>
Because the rules are in the Book, Check can test them:<P>
<PRE>
  check('sorted by company', book.list({ sort: 'company' })
    .map((c) =&gt; c.id), [ann.id, bob.id, 3]);
  check('search', book.list({ search: 'acorn' }).map(nameOf),
    ['Jones, Bob']);
</PRE>
Ann Smith has no company, so she comes first in company order, then Acorn, then Zebra Ltd.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Choosing the order</H2>
The order the user chose belongs to the whole application (a second window should use it too), so it goes in
the <CODE>app</CODE> object chapter 5 made for such things:<P>
<PRE>
export default async function start(task, ctx) {
  const app = {
    task,
    prefs: { sort: 'last' },         // the order of the list
    area: sprites.spritesFromFile(await vfs.readFile(ctx.dir +
      '.Sprites')),                  // the toolbar's pictures
  };
</PRE>
MainWindow gets two new keys and a way to change the order:<P>
<PRE>
  key(ev) {
    switch (ev.code) {
      case 0x184:                                             // F4
        wimp.setCaret(this.bar.pane, this.bar.search);
        return true;
      case 0x186: this.nextSort(); return true;               // F6
      case 1: this.list.selectAll(); return true;         // Ctrl-A
      case 26: this.list.clear(); return true;            // Ctrl-Z
      case 127: case 8: this.remove(); return true;       // Delete
      case 0x1CD: this.add(); return true;                // Insert
      default: return undefined;
    }
  }
  setSort(s) { this.app.prefs.sort = s; this.refresh(); }
  nextSort() {
    const keys = Object.keys(SORTS);
    const i = keys.indexOf(this.app.prefs.sort);
    this.setSort(keys[(i + 1) % keys.length]);
  }
</PRE>
<DL>
<DT>F4
<DD>Find: the caret goes into the Find field (<CODE>wimp.setCaret</CODE> with the pane and its search icon),
so you can type straight away. Return gives the keyboard back to the list.<P>
<DT>F6
<DD>Sort: the next order. <CODE>Object.keys(SORTS)</CODE> gives an array of an object's property names
(<CODE>Object.values</CODE> gives their values), so <CODE>nextSort</CODE> finds where the current order is in the
list of <CODE>SORTS</CODE>'s keys and moves one on; <CODE>% keys.length</CODE> goes back to the first after the
last.<P>
</DL>
And the menu gets a Sort submenu, with a tick against the current order, and a Find entry:<P>
<PRE>
      { text: 'Sort', submenu: new Menu('Sort', Object.entries(SORTS)
        .map(([k, name]) =&gt; ({ text: name,
          ticked: () =&gt; this.app.prefs.sort === k,
          action: () =&gt; this.setSort(k) }))) },
      { text: 'Find', key: 'F4',
        action: () =&gt; this.key({ code: 0x184 }) },
</PRE>
The submenu is made from <CODE>SORTS</CODE> with <CODE>map</CODE>, so adding an order to the Book adds it to
the menu. <CODE>ticked</CODE> is a function, like <CODE>shaded</CODE>, worked out as the menu is drawn. The
Find entry just does what F4 does, by handing <CODE>key</CODE> the same code. (The dotted line that separates
groups of entries has moved: it's now under <I>Clear selection</I>, the last of the selection entries, before
the new Sort and Find.)<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/C6Sort" WIDTH=629 HEIGHT=409></CENTER>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Trying it</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Run <CODE>!Contacts6</CODE> and press F6: the list is in order of first name. Press it twice more
to go through company order and back to surnames.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Press F4, type <CODE>work</CODE>: the people in the Work category. Type <CODE>01223</CODE> instead:
everyone with a Cambridge number.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Click Menu, and look at the Sort submenu: the current order is ticked.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Rules about the data - what matches, what order - belong in the model, where every part of the program
shares them and Check can test them. Views ask the model what to show.<P>
<LI>An object parameter with destructuring and defaults, <CODE>list({ search = '', sort = 'last' } = {})</CODE>,
gives named arguments.<P>
<LI><CODE>Object.entries</CODE> gives an object's <CODE>[name, value]</CODE> pairs; <CODE>some</CODE> is true
if any item passes a test; <CODE>([k, v]) =&gt;</CODE> destructures a pair.<P>
<LI>Sort with a compare function that compares keys in order and breaks ties; compare texts with
<CODE>localeCompare</CODE>.<P>
<LI>F4 is Find and F6 is Sort in the Style Guide. Settings that belong to the application go in
<CODE>app</CODE>.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Add a fourth order, by category (and then by name within each category).<P>
<DT><DD>2  Make Shift-F6 go through the orders backwards. (Shift adds &amp;10 to a function key's code.)<P>
<DT><DD>3  Some people would rather the Find field didn't look in email addresses. Leave them out.<P>
<DT><DD>4  Add a test to Check for first-name order.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Add it to <CODE>SORTS</CODE> - <CODE>category: 'Category'</CODE> - and a key in
<CODE>compare</CODE>:
<PRE>
: sort === 'category' ? [c.category, c.last, c.first]
</PRE>
The menu and F6 pick it up by themselves.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Give <CODE>nextSort</CODE> a direction, and add a case to <CODE>key</CODE>:
<PRE>
case 0x196: this.nextSort(-1); return true;   // Shift-F6
...
nextSort(d = 1) {
  const keys = Object.keys(SORTS);
  const i = keys.indexOf(this.app.prefs.sort);
  const n = keys.length;
  this.setSort(keys[(i + d + n) % n]);
}
</PRE>
(Adding <CODE>n</CODE> keeps the number from going below 0.)<P>
<DT><DD>3  <CODE>k !== 'id' &amp;&amp; k !== 'email' &amp;&amp; String(v)...</CODE> in
<CODE>matches</CODE>.<P>
<DT><DD>4  After the company test:
<PRE>
check('sorted by first name',
  book.list({ sort: 'first' }).map((c) =&gt; c.id),
  [3, ann.id, bob.id]);
</PRE>
Zebra Ltd, with no first name, comes first, then Ann, then Bob.<P>
</DL>

